{
  "dom_id": "step-q11550",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude \n $20$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude  $30.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a+b?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14253719/assets/3164bddbade652f3268832c5c3551f5e30263ceca95a3696d0da1ef5362853cb.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={20}^{2}+{30}^{2} \\\\  & =400+900 \\\\  & =1300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{1300}=10\\sqrt{13},\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{13}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
